= Columbia University — Disagreement Essay Prompt

“At Columbia, students representing a wide range of perspectives are invited to live and learn together. In such a community, questions and debates naturally arise. Please describe a time when you did not agree with someone and discuss how you engaged with them and what you took away from the interaction.”

_Word limit: 150 words or fewer._

== Raw Draft

September 22,2026 - *Again!*

I gave Aun four seconds before every rebuttal.
The longer I paused, the harder I tried to reason my way out of being wrong. I was no longer examining the argument; I was defending the person who had made it.

Our Discord community’s weekly stand-up turned into a courtroom, with Minecraft’s seed-generation algorithm on trial laid out my case: a seed’s integer should map to a defined set of properties, represented by an enum or struct.

Aun countered with "Bro is trying to serialize an ontology into a Rust enum and calling it procedural generation."

Perlin Noise...

Well... I had learned to defend an idea before learning how to doubt it.

Well, I had learned to defend an idea before learning how to doubt it. In retrospect, I might have questioned my claim from a third-person perspective. Solomon’s paradox, perhaps: the advice we can give others often eludes us when we are personally involved.

I let those impulses coexist without forcing a verdict cause some times verdict doesn't need announce it self and leaves perfect room for our mind to wonder for those co-existing thoughts.

I had been defending a representation before establishing that I understood the process it represented. Understanding Aun’s objection didn’t mean surrendering my position; it meant recognizing that I hadn’t yet earned the confidence with which I defended it.


