= Columbia Multidimensional and Collaborative Environment

Tell us about an aspect of your life so far or your lived experience that is important to you, and describe how it has shaped the way you would learn from and contribute to Columbia's multidimensional and collaborative environment. (150 words or fewer)

_Word limit: 150 words or fewer._


I suck;Doesn't everyone at first.

Learning the Sicilian Defense for the first time wasn’t as simple as staring at a notation sheet and memorizing the moves. It has many variations, such as the Najdorf Variation, which has been played by Bobby Fischer, Garry Kasparov, and Viswanathan Anand. Magnus Carlsen, however, stood out not because he favored a specific variation but because he frequently changed his perspective, adapting his thinking to the position before him.

At this moment of life,I've made peace with the beginner's pain cause its natural cause learning everyday requires this kind of pain tolerance.Well... learning means enduring the discomfort of not knowing, again and again.

At Columbia, I'll bring willingness into unfamiliar communities, whether by joining one or starting one myself. I'll listen, let different perspectives unsettle my assumptions, and offer ideas that others can challenge and build upon. I may not know the next move, but I'm willing to stay at the board.

